. Update dependency (pom.xml) of services to include OpenTelemetry (a Java agent)

pom.xml


<dependency>
	<groupId>io.opentelemetry.javaagent</groupId>
	<artifactId>opentelemetry-javaagent</artifactId>
	<version>${otelVersion}</version>
	<scope>runtime</scope>
</dependency>


. Update application.yml to log out tag, trace ID and span ID generated by OpenTelemetry

application.yml


# Update package according to your package name
logging:
  level:
    com:
      samsung:
        spp:
          device:
            collection: DEBUG
  pattern:
    level: "%5p [${spring.application.name},%X{trace_id},%X{span_id}]"


. Add environment variable for OpenTelemetry configuration (update deployment yaml file in case of deploy with Kubernetes or Docker-compose.yaml file in case of Docker Compose deployment).

deployment.yaml


      containers:
        - name: data-collection
          image: sp1p-docker-local.bart.sec.samsung.net/neo/data-collection:v1.0.4
          imagePullPolicy: Always
          ports:
            - containerPort: 9001
          env:
            - name: JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS
              value: "-javaagent:/app/libs/opentelemetry-javaagent-2.16.0.jar"
            - name: OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT
              value: "http://tempo.monitoring.svc.cluster.local:4318"
            - name: OTEL_METRICS_EXPORTER
              value: none
            - name: OTEL_LOGS_EXPORTER
              value: none
            - name: OTEL_SERVICE_NAME
              value: "data-collection"
The value "http://tempo.monitoring.svc.cluster.local:4318" is the endpoint of Tempo to receive trace data from OpenTelemetry.

It should be changed if Tempo is deployed at a different endpoint.



. Install Tempo

.. Chart: tempo-1.21.1.tgz

.. Values file: values-tempo-srv.yaml


Install Tempo using Helm


helm install tempo ./tempo-1.21.1.tgz -f values-tempo-srv.yaml --namespace monitoring

# Check deployed Helm
helm ls -n monitoring
